{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785183897,"nonce":"qslSKv8yhAU9hGbmbqKn6550nic=","title":"ADR-0062 (HITL): decide automatic Cache detection for lockfile-derived trees — needs its own ADR","message":"Recognise `node_modules`, `.venv`, vendored `~/.m2` and the pip/uv caches from their lockfiles and route them through ADR-0007's **Cache** instead of the Workspace, so less is subject to the write cost at all. Keeps the knowledge in the engine rather than in authors' YAML, which is the right side of ADR-0061's governing principle.\n\n**Deliberately excluded from ADR-0062** and needs its own trade-off record, because its failure mode is *wrong output*: the split is behavioural, not nameable. `target/` is Cache-shaped when the next Step rebuilds and an **output** when the next Step consumes a built binary. Guess \"cache\" on the second case and a pipeline breaks silently.\n\n\n- [ ] An ADR that states exactly which trees are safe to detect and why, and what makes the rest unsafe\n- [ ] The safe set is derived from a declared input (a lockfile), so a miss is slower and never wrong\n- [ ] No heuristic is shipped that can drop a tree a downstream Step consumes\n\n\nNone - decision first, no code","files":null}]}